<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Bari</h1><p class="st-meta">1119 CE – 1132 CE · capital Bari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived Lombard principality carved out of Norman Apulia by a local strongman before Roger II brought it to heel.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In the weak reign of Duke William II of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria">Duchy of Apulia and Calabria</a>, the citizen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grimoald-alferanites">Grimoald Alferanites</a> took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> in 1119 and called himself prince. Bari, the old seat of Byzantine government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, had since 1087 housed the relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, which made it a major pilgrimage centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grimoald-alferanites">Grimoald</a>'s government beyond his alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and with other rebellious Apulian barons. He relied on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari's</a> port, its merchant community and the prestige of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-san-nicola">Basilica of San Nicola</a>. He submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> in 1129 when Roger took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grimoald-alferanites">Grimoald</a> joined a baronial revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a>, who captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> in 1132 and sent him as a prisoner to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Bari was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1119 CE – 1132 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.97%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1119 CE – 1132 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Grimoald Alferanites</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italo-Romance, Greek, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bari (1119–1132)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1126&amp;focus=principality-of-bari" aria-label="Principality of Bari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5092 710 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Bari lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5412 966L5383 971M5412 966L5333 1064"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5383 971h0M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5412 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5412 966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1126&amp;focus=principality-of-bari">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
